#: ../../../xonsh_projects.rst:5
msgid "Xonsh Projects"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../xonsh_projects.rst:6
msgid ""
"Bam! Suppose you want to get beyond scripting and write a whole library, "
"utility, or other big project in xonsh. Here is how you do that. Spoiler "
"alert: it is easy, powerful, and fun!"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../xonsh_projects.rst:11
msgid "Overview"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../xonsh_projects.rst:12
msgid ""
"Xonsh is fully interoperable with Python. Writing a xonsh library is very "
"similar to writing a Python library, using all of the same tooling and "
"infrastructure for packaging pure Python code."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../xonsh_projects.rst:17
msgid "Structure"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../xonsh_projects.rst:18
msgid ""
"Xonsh modules are written in xonsh files (``*.xsh``), side-by-side with "
"Python files (``*.py``). Suppose we have a package called ``mypkg`` which "
"uses xonsh files. Here is a sample file system layout would be::"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../xonsh_projects.rst:22
msgid ""
"|- mypkg/\n"
"   |- __init__.py    # a regular package with an init file\n"
"   |- other.py       # not a xonsh file\n"
"   |- show.py        # \"mypkg.show\", full module name\n"
"   |- tell.xsh       # \"mypkg.tell\", full module name\n"
"   |- subpkg/\n"
"      |- __init__.py\n"
"      |- a.py      # \"mypkg.subpkg.a\", full module name\n"
"      |- b.xsh     # \"mypkg.subpkg.b\", full module name"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../xonsh_projects.rst:32
msgid ""
"To ensure that these files are installed, you need to declare the ``*.xsh`` "
"files as package data in your project's ``pyproject.toml``. For the above "
"structure, this looks like the following."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../xonsh_projects.rst:36
msgid ""
"[build-system]\n"
"requires = [\"setuptools>=61\"]\n"
"build-backend = \"setuptools.build_meta\"\n"
"\n"
"[project]\n"
"name = \"mypkg\"\n"
"version = \"0.1.0\"\n"
"\n"
"[tool.setuptools]\n"
"packages = [\"mypkg\", \"mypkg.subpkg\"]\n"
"\n"
"[tool.setuptools.package-data]\n"
"mypkg = [\"*.xsh\"]\n"
"\"mypkg.subpkg\" = [\"*.xsh\"]"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../xonsh_projects.rst:53
msgid ""
"With this, the xonsh code will be installed and included in any source "
"distribution you create!"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../xonsh_projects.rst:57
msgid "Setting up xonsh sessions"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../xonsh_projects.rst:58
msgid ""
"Xonsh code requires a ``XonshSession`` to exist as ``builtins.__xonsh__`` "
"and for be that object to be setup correctly. This can be quite a bit of "
"work and the exact setup depends on the execution context. To simplify the "
"process of constructing the session properly, xonsh provides the ``xonsh."
"main.setup()`` function specifically for use in 3rd party packages."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../xonsh_projects.rst:64
msgid ""
"While ``xonsh.main.setup()`` is safely re-entrant, it is a good idea to add "
"the following snippet to the root-level ``__init__.py`` of your project. "
"With the ``mypkg`` example above, the session setup is as follows:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../xonsh_projects.rst:68
msgid "``mypkg/__init__.py``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../xonsh_projects.rst:70
msgid ""
"from xonsh.main import setup\n"
"setup()\n"
"del setup"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../xonsh_projects.rst:76
msgid "Enjoy!"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../xonsh_projects.rst:81
msgid "Formatting xonsh code"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../xonsh_projects.rst:82
msgid ""
"Xonsh ships a built-in formatter, available as the ``xonsh format`` "
"subcommand. It re-emits the source with normalized indentation, spacing and "
"blank-line rules while preserving every xonsh-specific construct."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../xonsh_projects.rst:87
msgid "Format a single file in place:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../xonsh_projects.rst:89
msgid "@ xonsh format mypkg/tell.xsh"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../xonsh_projects.rst:93
msgid ""
"Format several files at once — every path that needs reformatting is "
"rewritten on disk, and a status line is printed for each:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../xonsh_projects.rst:96
msgid "@ xonsh format mypkg/tell.xsh mypkg/subpkg/b.xsh"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../xonsh_projects.rst:100
msgid ""
"Read from standard input and write the result to standard output — the "
"canonical way to produce a separate output file or to plug the formatter "
"into an editor / pipeline:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../xonsh_projects.rst:104
msgid "@ xonsh format - < 1.xsh > 2.xsh"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../xonsh_projects.rst:108
msgid "Useful flags:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../xonsh_projects.rst:110
msgid ""
"``--check`` — don't touch any files. Exits with code ``1`` if at least one "
"file would be reformatted, ``0`` otherwise. Handy for CI."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../xonsh_projects.rst:112
msgid ""
"``--diff`` — print a unified diff for each file that would change, again "
"without touching disk."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../xonsh_projects.rst:114
msgid "``-q`` / ``--quiet`` — suppress per-file status messages on stderr."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../xonsh_projects.rst:116
msgid "For example, to fail a CI job when any file needs reformatting:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../xonsh_projects.rst:118
msgid "@ xonsh format --check mypkg"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../xonsh_projects.rst:124
msgid "CLI app on Xonsh"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../xonsh_projects.rst:126
msgid ""
"Building a command-line application on Xonsh is easy: you write and package "
"it like any other Python project, so it is installable, testable, and "
"distributable out of the box. For a basic implementation to start from, see "
"the `xonsh-awesome-cli-app`_ template — fork it and add your own commands."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../xonsh_projects.rst:131
msgid ""
"For commands that live inside a session rather than a standalone app, Xonsh "
"also ships built-in :ref:`Click CLI Integration <click_cli_integration>` "
"that registers a Click command as an alias."
msgstr ""
